LR  - 20220408
IS  - 1939-1315 (Electronic)
IS  - 0022-3514 (Linking)
VI  - 104
IP  - 4
DP  - 2013 Apr
TI  - Rethinking stress: the role of mindsets in determining the stress response.
PG  - 716-33
LID - 10.1037/a0031201 [doi]
AB  - This article describes 3 studies that explore the role of mindsets in the context
      of stress. In Study 1, we present data supporting the reliability and validity of
      an 8-item instrument, the Stress Mindset Measure (SMM), designed to assess the
      extent to which an individual believes that the effects of stress are either
      enhancing or debilitating. In Study 2, we demonstrate that stress mindsets can be
      altered by watching short, multimedia film clips presenting factual information
      biased toward defining the nature of stress in 1 of 2 ways (stress-is-enhancing
      vs. stress-is-debilitating). In Study 3, we demonstrate the effect of stress
      mindset on physiological and behavioral outcomes, showing that a
      stress-is-enhancing mindset is associated with moderate cortisol reactivity and
      high desire for feedback under stress. Together, these 3 studies suggest that
      stress mindset is a distinct and meaningful variable in determining the stress
      response.
