TI  - The Influence of Race and Ethnicity on the Relationship between Family Resilience
      and Parenting Stress in Caregivers of Children with Autism.
PG  - 650-658
LID - 10.1007/s10803-019-04269-6 [doi]
AB  - We examined the relationship between family resilience and parenting stress among
      parents of children with autism spectrum disorder, with a specific focus on
      race/ethnicity as a moderator. Multivariate models indicated that family
      resilience was associated with parenting stress. Race/ethnicity significantly
      moderated the relationship between family resilience and parenting stress. The
      effects of family resilience on parenting stress were significantly different
      among parents of African American, Hispanic, and white children. These effects
      were strongest for parents of African American children. Compared to white and
      Hispanic children, parents of African American children with low levels of family
      resilience had 60-82% higher probability of parenting stress; while those with
      high levels of family resilience had 15-18% lower probability for parenting
      stress.
