TI  - Resilience to suicide ideation: A cross-cultural test of the buffering
      hypothesis.
PG  - e1-e9
LID - 10.1002/cpp.2118 [doi]
AB  - Depression and suicide ideation are common in student populations across the
      world. The present study investigated factors buffering the association between
      depression and suicide ideation. A total of 2,687 Chinese students and 601 German
      students took part in the investigation. Social support, satisfaction with life,
      self-efficacy, psychosocial stress resistance, and positive mental health were
      considered as resilience factors moderating the association between depressive
      symptoms and suicide ideation within both samples. Positive mental health
      moderated the impact of depressive symptoms on suicide ideation in German and
      Chinese students. Life satisfaction moderated the impact of depressive symptoms
      on suicide ideation in German students. Social support moderated the impact of
      depressive symptoms on suicide ideation in Chinese students. No interaction
      effects were found for self-efficacy and psychosocial stress resistance. Positive
      mental health, satisfaction with life, and perceived social support seem to
      confer resilience and should be taken into account, when assessing individuals
      for suicide risk.
