DP  - 2019 Oct
TI  - The strength of pursuing your values: Valued living as a path to resilience among
      college students.
PG  - 532-541
LID - 10.1002/smi.2886 [doi]
AB  - Although stressful life events (SLEs) frequently relate to negative outcomes,
      many individuals demonstrate resilience by positively adapting to stressors
      without significant impairment. Valued living, a key process in the theory
      underpinning acceptance and commitment therapy is a response style characterized
      by daily purposeful actions that are linked to important values and may promote
      resilience in college students. This study examined the main and interactive
      effects of SLEs and valued living in the prediction of a multidimensional measure
      of resilience. Results supported a strong positive relationship between
      resilience and valued living. Further, valued living moderated the association
      between SLE negative impact scores and resilience (but not the association
      between cumulative prior year SLEs and resilience). Although SLE negative impact
      scores were negatively associated with resilience at lower levels of valued
      living, there was no demonstrated relationship at higher levels of valued living.
      Implications related to promoting valued living among college students in the
      service of increasing resilience are discussed.
