TI  - Prospective trajectories of posttraumatic stress in college women following a
      campus mass shooting.
PG  - 249-56
LID - 10.1002/jts.21914 [doi]
AB  - In a sample with known levels of preshooting posttraumatic stress (PTS) symptoms,
      we examined the impact of a campus mass shooting on trajectories of PTS in the 31
      months following the shooting using latent growth mixture modeling. Female
      students completed 7 waves of a longitudinal study (sample sizes ranged from 812
      to 559). We identified 4 distinct trajectories (n = 660): (a) minimal
      impact-resilience (60.9%), (b) high impact-recovery (29.1%), (c) moderate
      impact-moderate symptoms (8.2%), and (d) chronic dysfunction (1.8%). Individuals
      in each trajectory class remained at or returned to preshooting levels of PTS
      approximately 6 months postshooting. The minimal impact-resilience class reported
      less prior trauma exposure (η(2) = .13), less shooting exposure (η(2) = .07), and
      greater emotion regulation skills than all other classes (η(2) > .30). The
      chronic dysfunction class endorsed higher rates of experiential avoidance prior
      to the shooting than the minimal-impact resilient and high impact-recovery
      classes (η(2) = .15), as well as greater shooting exposure than the high
      impact-recovery class (η(2) = .07). Findings suggest that preshooting functioning
      and emotion regulation distinguish between those who experience prolonged
      distress following mass violence and those who gradually recover.
