TI  - Health-related quality of life and adaptive behaviors of adolescents with sickle
      cell disease: stress processing moderators.
PG  - 335-44
LID - 10.1007/s10880-011-9254-3 [doi]
AB  - The objective of this study was to examine resilience among adolescents with
      sickle cell disease (SCD), focusing on the interaction of health-related quality
      of life with stress processing to explain adaptive behavior. Forty-four
      adolescents with SCD completed paper-and-pencil measures of health-related
      quality of life, appraisals (hope), pain coping strategies (e.g. adherence), and
      adaptive behavior. Self-reported health-related quality of life was significantly
      associated with adaptive behavior, as was adherence. Findings for moderation were
      mixed. Pain coping strategies moderated the association of health-related quality
      of life with adaptive behavior such that at lower levels of Coping Strategies
      Questionnaire (CSQ) Adherence, better quality of life was associated with higher
      adaptive behavior. Similarly, at higher levels of hope, better quality of life
      was associated with higher adaptive behavior, and poorer quality of life was
      associated with lower adaptive behavior. Adolescents with SCD showed resilience,
      particularly in terms of personal adjustment, that may be explained by their
      appraisals and stress processing strategies. Interventions to support an
      optimistic or hopeful outlook and improve adherence to recommendations for
      medical management of sickle cell pain may result in improved resilience/adaptive
      behavior.
