TI  - Burnout and psychological distress amongst Australian healthcare workers during
      the COVID-19 pandemic.
PG  - 26-30
LID - 10.1177/1039856220965045 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To examine psychological distress in healthcare workers (HCWs) during
      the COVID-19 pandemic in April-May 2020. METHODS: A cross-sectional survey
      examining demographic, employment and mental health characteristics of HCWs in a
      large metropolitan hospital in Australia. RESULTS: HCWs showed significant
      symptoms of moderate-severe level depression (21%), anxiety (20%) and
      posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD; 29%), associated with burnout, prior
      psychiatric history, profession and resilience. CONCLUSION: Despite low levels of
      COVID contact, moderate to high levels of psychological distress were reported.
      Continued monitoring and support for HCWs' mental well-being is warranted as the
      COVID-19 pandemic develops.
